# Line endings.
#
# The repo is developed on Windows and deployed into Linux containers, and git's autocrlf gave the
# working tree CRLF for everything. That is invisible for Python and fatal for a shell script: the
# image builds fine, then `sh scripts/install-plugin-deps.sh` fails with
#
#     scripts/install-plugin-deps.sh: 5: : not found
#     scripts/install-plugin-deps.sh: 6: set: Illegal option -
#
# because sh reads `set -e\r` and the \r is part of the argument. Measured on the data-centre build
# of the CamoFox image, where it stopped the C3 render tier from being built at all.
#
# So: anything a container EXECUTES is pinned to LF regardless of platform. Everything else keeps
# whatever git decides — a Python file with CRLF has never cost anything.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
entrypoint*     text eol=lf
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
